Finding: {"title":"Part status 'expired' is dead code — branch order bug hides expired shelf-life parts as 'expiring_soon'","severity":"MEDIUM","area":"Business Logic / Airworthiness Correctness","files":["src/server/routes/routes-parts.ts"],"detail":"`deriveStatus()` (lines 58-77) checks `if (daysUntilExpiry <= 30) return 'expiring_soon';` BEFORE `if (daysUntilExpiry < 0) return 'expired';`. Since any negative number is also `<= 30`, the expired branch can never be reached — an already-expired part is always classified as 'expiring_soon'. The frontend (`part-detail.tsx` PartStatus type, `parts-scanner.tsx` STATUS_CFG) fully defines and displays a distinct 'expired' status (red, distinguishable from amber 'expiring_soon'), but the backend can never emit it. The GET /parts summary object (lines 147-153) also omits an `expired` count entirely, consistent with this dead branch.","impact":"Shelf-life-expired parts (which may be unusable/unairworthy per aviation parts traceability requirements) are visually indistinguishable from parts merely approaching expiry, understating urgency to mechanics/inventory staff reviewing the parts list.","recommendation":"Swap the branch order: check `daysUntilExpiry < 0` (expired) before `daysUntilExpiry <= 30` (expiring_soon). Also add an `expired` key to the GET /parts summary aggregation to match the frontend's PartsSummary expectations used elsewhere (parts-scanner.tsx already expects `summary.expired`).","confidence":"high"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

